<section class="section" id="conclusions">
<h2>6. Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

<h3>Key Findings</h3>
<ol class="insight-list">
  <li><strong>The BIEK Computer Science exam has NOT remained structurally stable</strong> across the 2015–2025 period. While MCQ and Long question counts are constant, Short questions underwent two significant shifts — doubling from 9 to 18 in 2017, then reducing to 15 in 2021.</li>
  <li><strong>The exam has stabilized since 2021</strong> — the current format of 15 MCQs + 15 Short + 3 Long (33 total) has persisted for five consecutive years, suggesting this is the new equilibrium.</li>
  <li><strong>Topic coverage is remarkably consistent.</strong> C Programming and Database concepts are the twin pillars of the exam, appearing every year across all question types. Sub-topics like loops, operators, keys, and MS Access objects are the most predictable components.</li>
  <li><strong>Recent years show slight topic broadening.</strong> File handling, structures, pointers, and advanced database concepts (ACID properties, ER diagrams, views) have appeared more frequently in 2023–2025, possibly reflecting syllabus evolution.</li>
  <li><strong>MCQs are the most predictable section.</strong> With a fixed count of 15 and recurring topic categories (data types, operators, format specifiers, database terminology), the MCQ section offers the highest return on targeted preparation.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Limitations</h3>
<div class="callout">
  <ol class="insight-list">
    <li><strong>Missing 2020 data:</strong> No examination data is available for 2020, likely due to COVID-19. This creates a gap in the trend analysis and makes it impossible to determine whether the 2019→2021 structural change was abrupt or gradual.</li>
    <li><strong>No annual vs. supplementary distinction:</strong> The dataset does not distinguish between annual and supplementary examination papers. If both are included for some years, counts may reflect more than one paper per year.</li>
    <li><strong>Community-compiled data:</strong> This is not an official BIEK dataset. Minor transcription errors may exist in question text.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyword-based topic classification:</strong> Topic analysis relies on regular expression matching, which may misclassify some questions or miss topics expressed in unexpected phrasing. A question can match multiple topics, so topic counts are not mutually exclusive.</li>
    <li><strong>Small dataset:</strong> With 327 total questions, fine-grained statistical analysis (e.g., per-subtopic year-over-year significance testing) has limited power.</li>
    <li><strong>Dollar-sign delimiter:</strong> The <code>$</code> delimiter in the question column separates MCQ options and Long question sub-parts. Topic analysis was performed on full question text including these sub-elements, which may inflate some topic frequencies.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<p style="margin-top: 24px; color: var(--text-light); font-size: 0.9em;">Data source: BIEK Computer Science 12th Class Past Papers (2015–2025), community-compiled question bank. Original source: <a href="https://www.biek.edu.pk/" target="_blank">BIEK official website</a> and publicly available scanned papers.</p>
</section>

